cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Assevra or report a score measured with it, please cite this project."
title: "Assevra: A Reliability Scorecard for LLM Agents"
abstract: >-
  Assevra is an open reference implementation and a named methodology (the
  Assevra Reliability Scorecard) that turns agent test runs into signed,
  statistically defensible release evidence. It scores an agent across nine
  dimensions - grounding/faithfulness, safety/refusal, PII-leak, task-completion,
  tool-call validation, action correctness, prompt-injection resistance, cost, and
  latency - each with an explicit scoring method (deterministic where a rule can
  decide it, LLM-as-judge only where judgment is irreducible), a stated pass
  threshold, and a 95% Wilson confidence interval reported alongside the sample
  size. It also reports pass^k and run-to-run consistency over repeated trials.
  The output is a portable, schema-governed artifact that can be cryptographically
  signed and mapped to AI-governance control families.
